(1869662, 252308, 6999, '<td valign="top"><b>from the darkened room of the <u>Critique Club</u></b> <br/> <br/>Whoa, you got hammered on this one, no? I''m guessing you know the problem, certainly as far as the challenge topic was concerned - the invisibility of that wagging tail. I''ve really no idea how to siuggest an approach that would fix that - this isn''t my field at all, clever things with shutter speeds, and all I can say is that you''d surely ideally need a very bright object, or at least brighter than it''s surroundings, to leave a trail. Sam''s tail appears largely black, and so that''s just going to be invisible against any kind of light.<br/> <br/>One element I think might repay some attention is that of saturation in your shot. Now - I think you''ve caught a certain kind of autumnal fading of light very well, especially whilst maintaining such detail in the dog''s face, and there is also that suggesting of the bluness of the disappearing afternoon light which I find very atmospheric. From the file size and general sharpness, I have no doubts you''re completely in control of what you''re doing; but I''d bet you''d get a little more reaction from the general voter with more colour punch to your shots (or even perhaps less - black and white?) but with more contrast. Had you been able to show more richness in those greens and browns of the grass and leaves, I bet you''d have got a few more views - whether that would have attracted higher votes is another matter.</td>', 'from the darkened room of the Critique Club  Whoa, you got hammered on this one, no? I''m guessing you know the problem, certainly as far as the challenge topic was concerned - the invisibility of that wagging tail. I''ve really no idea how to siuggest an approach that would fix that - this isn''t my field at all, clever things with shutter speeds, and all I can say is that you''d surely ideally need a very bright object, or at least brighter than it''s surroundings, to leave a trail. Sam''s tail appears largely black, and so that''s just going to be invisible against any kind of light. One element I think might repay some attention is that of saturation in your shot. Now - I think you''ve caught a certain kind of autumnal fading of light very well, especially whilst maintaining such detail in the dog''s face, and there is also that suggesting of the bluness of the disappearing afternoon light which I find very atmospheric. From the file size and general sharpness, I have no doubts you''re completely in control of what you''re doing; but I''d bet you''d get a little more reaction from the general voter with more colour punch to your shots (or even perhaps less - black and white?) but with more contrast. Had you been able to show more richness in those greens and browns of the grass and leaves, I bet you''d have got a few more views - whether that would have attracted higher votes is another matter.', '2005-11-15 18:54:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(1870786, 251934, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Wow what a capture! I remember seeing this image, personally I would have thought it would have been top 20, but 28 isn''t too bad either.  The action and timing are prefect.  I like the fact the subject is not centered is prefect for me, I really hate when people center.  The waves look prefect not overwhelming and not boring.  I really cannot see anything I would change or attempt to improve.  I see a little blotchyness in the left upper side but it is hardly noticeable.  You did an excellent job.</td>', '*Critique Club* Wow what a capture! I remember seeing this image, personally I would have thought it would have been top 20, but 28 isn''t too bad either.  The action and timing are prefect.  I like the fact the subject is not centered is prefect for me, I really hate when people center.  The waves look prefect not overwhelming and not boring.  I really cannot see anything I would change or attempt to improve.  I see a little blotchyness in the left upper side but it is hardly noticeable.  You did an excellent job.', '2005-11-15 23:53:56.000000', NULL, 0),
